  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Gulfstream Aerospace recorded $313M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Gulfstream Aerospace won 79 awards this fiscal year so far, with an average award size of $4M, and obligations are up 134.9% year over year. Gulfstream Aerospace obligations have varied across recent fiscal years: FY21 $206M, FY22 $115M, FY23 $119M, FY24 $225M, FY25 $133M, and FY26 $313M. The largest buying agencies so far are the Department of Homeland Security with $181M and the Department of Defense with $130M, followed by the Department of Justice with $1.9M. The largest NAICS markets for Gulfstream Aerospace in FY2026 to date are Commercial Air, Rail, and Water Transportation Equipment Rental and Leasing at $184M and Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $103M. Gulfstream Aerospace also shows activity in Aircraft Manufacturing at $26.8M and smaller categories such as Software Publishers at $140K and All Other Publishers at $67.2K. Current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Gulfstream Aerospace's FY2026 federal obligations?",
        "body": "Gulfstream Aerospace obligations are concentrated in a few large buyers and markets. The Department of Homeland Security accounts for $181M and the Department of Defense accounts for $130M. The Commercial Air, Rail, and Water Transportation Equipment Rental and Leasing market accounts for $184M and Other Support Activities for Air Transportation accounts for $103M. Several smaller markets and agency entries make up the remainder."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How have Gulfstream Aerospace obligations moved over recent fiscal years?",
        "body": "Gulfstream Aerospace obligations rose to $313M in FY26 from $133M in FY25, following year-to-year fluctuations: FY21 $206M, FY22 $115M, FY23 $119M, FY24 $225M, FY25 $133M, FY26 $313M. Gulfstream Aerospace obligations in FY2026 to date are up 134.9% year over year. The current-year totals are partial and reflect fiscal year to date obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Gulfstream Aerospace make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Gulfstream Aerospace has $313M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Gulfstream Aerospace won 79 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $4M, and obligations are up 134.9% year over year. The FY-by-FY obligations series is FY21 $206M, FY22 $115M, FY23 $119M, FY24 $225M, FY25 $133M, FY26 $313M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Gulfstream Aerospace?",
      "a": "The Department of Homeland Security and the Department of Defense are Gulfstream Aerospace's largest buyers in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $181M and the Department of Defense obligated $130M, followed by the Department of Justice at $1.9M, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $152K, and the Department of Transportation at $45.6K."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Gulfstream Aerospace sell to the government?",
      "a": "Gulfstream Aerospace sells aviation-related goods and services to the government, concentrated in transport and support markets. The largest NAICS markets in FY2026 to date are Commercial Air, Rail, and Water Transportation Equipment Rental and Leasing at $184M, Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $103M, and Aircraft Manufacturing at $26.8M, with smaller activity in Software Publishers at $140K and All Other Publishers at $67.2K."
    },
